            id: 'rd_1', title: 'Reading Test 1: The Impact of Microplastics on Marine Life',
            text: '<p class="mb-4">Microplastics, defined as plastic fragments smaller than five millimeters, have become one of the most pervasive pollutants in the world\'s oceans. These tiny particles originate from a variety of sources, including the breakdown of larger plastic debris, microbeads in cosmetics, and synthetic fibers washed from clothing. Because of their microscopic size, they are easily ingested by a wide range of marine organisms, from zooplankton to large filter-feeding whales.</p><p class="mb-4">The ingestion of microplastics poses severe physical and chemical threats to marine life. Physically, these particles can block the digestive tracts of small fish, leading to starvation and death. Chemically, microplastics act as sponges, absorbing toxic chemicals such as pesticides and heavy metals from the surrounding seawater. When marine animals consume these contaminated particles, the toxins accumulate in their tissues, a process known as bioaccumulation.</p><p class="mb-4">Furthermore, this issue extends beyond marine ecosystems and directly affects human health. As larger predators consume smaller prey that have ingested microplastics, the concentration of toxins magnifies up the food chain. Consequently, humans who consume seafood are unwittingly ingesting these harmful substances, which have been linked to hormonal disruptions and other health issues.</p>',
