                "title": "Reading Test 3: Modern Sleep Patterns",
                "text": "<p class=\"mb-4\">The way humans sleep has changed dramatically over the centuries. Before the advent of artificial lighting, our ancestors likely experienced segmented sleep, waking in the middle of the night for a few hours before returning to slumber. Today, the expectation of an uninterrupted eight-hour block of sleep is a relatively modern construct, heavily influenced by the industrial revolution and the need for standardized working hours.</p><p class=\"mb-4\">However, this modern pattern is increasingly disrupted by the demands of contemporary life. The omnipresence of screens emitting blue light suppresses the production of melatonin, a hormone crucial for sleep regulation. Furthermore, the 24/7 nature of a globalized economy and the constant connectivity facilitated by smartphones have blurred the lines between work and rest, leading to a pervasive epidemic of sleep deprivation.</p><p class=\"mb-4\">The consequences of chronic sleep deprivation are profound. It impairs cognitive function, weakens the immune system, and increases the risk of chronic conditions such as obesity and cardiovascular disease. Recognizing the importance of sleep hygiene—establishing a consistent sleep schedule and creating a restful environment—is essential for mitigating these risks and improving overall well-being.</p>",
