                "id": "rd_13",
                "title": "Reading Test 13: Ocean Acidification",
                "text": "<p class=\"mb-4\">Often referred to as climate change's \"evil twin,\" ocean acidification is a severe environmental threat driven by the alarming increase in atmospheric carbon dioxide (CO2). As the ocean absorbs approximately thirty percent of the CO2 released into the atmosphere, a series of chemical reactions occur, lowering the water's pH and increasing its acidity. This fundamental shift in oceanic chemistry has profound implications for marine ecosystems globally.</p><p class=\"mb-4\">The most immediate victims of this acidification are calcifying organisms, such as oysters, clams, sea urchins, and corals. These creatures rely on carbonate ions to build their calcium carbonate shells and skeletons. As acidity increases, carbonate ions become less abundant, making it significantly more difficult for these organisms to maintain their protective structures. In severe cases, existing shells can actually begin to dissolve in the corrosive water.</p><p class=\"mb-4\">The impact extends far beyond these individual species. Coral reefs, which harbor a quarter of all marine biodiversity, are particularly vulnerable. The degradation of reefs threatens the complex food webs they support, ultimately jeopardizing the livelihoods of millions of people who depend on fisheries and coastal tourism. Addressing this crisis requires drastic and immediate reductions in global carbon emissions.</p>",
