                "title": "Reading Test 18: Roman Concrete Secrets",
                "text": "<p class=\"mb-4\">The architectural legacy of the Roman Empire is legendary, with structures like the Pantheon and the Colosseum standing intact for over two millennia. This astonishing durability is largely attributed to the unique composition of Roman concrete (opus caementicium). Unlike modern concrete, which often degrades and crumbles within a few decades, especially in marine environments, Roman concrete actually grows stronger over time.</p><p class=\"mb-4\">For years, the exact recipe remained a mystery. Recent chemical analyses revealed that the Romans utilized a specific mixture of volcanic ash (pozzolana), lime, and seawater. When this mixture was exposed to the ocean, a rare chemical reaction occurred, prompting the growth of interlocking minerals like tobermorite. These minerals effectively sealed existing cracks and reinforced the internal structure, giving the material an incredible self-healing capability.</p><p class=\"mb-4\">Modern engineers are intensely studying these ancient techniques to develop more sustainable and durable building materials. Producing modern Portland cement requires extremely high temperatures, contributing significantly to global carbon emissions. By incorporating volcanic ash and adopting the self-healing principles of Roman concrete, the modern construction industry could drastically reduce its environmental footprint and create longer-lasting infrastructure.</p>",
